+
+# Publish scheduled posts by the clock
+
+## Context
+
+Authors write ahead of a launch, but a post went live the moment it was
+saved. Finished drafts were kept elsewhere and pasted in at the right minute,
+and one launch post went out a day early. Editorial asked for a publish date
+on a post; ops asked for a report of what is scheduled.
+
+## Decision
+
+`posts.published_at` is a nullable text column. `NULL`, or a time that has
+passed, means the post is public; a later time means it is scheduled.
+
+- **Nothing runs at publication time.** There is no job and no `published`
+  flag to flip: every read compares `published_at` with the current time.
+  `Post.visibleTo(viewerId)`, `Post.scope('published')` and
+  `Post.scope('scheduled')` are the only places that rule is written.
+- **The column is compared as text.** The app only ever writes
+  `Date#toISOString()` output (UTC, millisecond precision), whatever offset the
+  request carried, and in that format text order is time order. The ORM's
+  query builder has no raw SQL, so this is what lets the filter, the
+  pagination count and the eager loads stay one query.
+- **A scheduled post is a 404 to everyone but its author**, on every route
+  that takes its id, edit, update and delete included. Those look the post up
+  through `Post.visibleTo()` before authorizing: the 403 the policy would give
+  confirms that the post exists.
+- **The request field is `publishAt`, and it needs an offset.** Absent or
+  `null` means "publish now", on an edit as well: a `PUT` that leaves the
+  field out clears the date. The edit form therefore always sends the stored
+  value back. A date-time without an offset is rejected rather than read in
+  the server's time zone; the form converts from the author's local time.
+- **The home page lists published posts only**, for the author too. Their
+  scheduled posts are in the posts list and in search, marked with the date.
+- **`posts:scheduled` prints the report and nothing else on stdout**, one
+  `<id>\t<title>\t<published_at>` line per post. Tabs and line breaks inside a
+  title are printed as a space. `bin/console.ts` waits for stdout and stderr
+  to be written out before `process.exit()`, which used to cut off anything a
+  slower reader on the pipe had not taken yet.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Publication is exact to the request and cannot be missed by a worker that
+  was down; moving `published_at` in the database is all it takes to
+  reschedule a post.
+- A value written to `published_at` by hand has to be in the same format.
+  Another spelling of a valid time (`2026-10-01 09:00:00`, an offset other
+  than `Z`) is compared as text all the same and can be off by up to the
+  difference between the two spellings.
+- A client that predates the field and edits a scheduled post publishes it,
+  since leaving `publishAt` out clears the date.
+- Anything that reads posts for the public has to go through the scopes
+  above. `Post.newQuery()` and `Post.find()` see scheduled posts.
+- There is no event at the moment a post goes live, so notifying anyone then
+  would need a scheduler after all.
diff --git a/resources/js/components/PublishAtField.tsx b/resources/js/components/PublishAtField.tsx
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..5332895
--- /dev/null
+++ b/resources/js/components/PublishAtField.tsx
@@ -0,0 +1,64 @@
+import { useEffect, useState } from 'react'
+
+interface Props {
+  /** ISO 8601 in UTC, as the server stores and validates it; null publishes now. */
+  value: string | null | undefined
+  onChange: (value: string | null) => void
+  error?: string
+  className?: string
+}
+
+// <input type="datetime-local"> speaks `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:mm` in the viewer's own
+// time zone and knows nothing about offsets, so the conversion happens here
+// and the form only ever holds what the server expects.
+function toInputValue(value: string | null | undefined): string {
+  if (!value) {
+    return ''
+  }
+
+  const date = new Date(value)
+  if (Number.isNaN(date.getTime())) {
+    return ''
+  }
+
+  const local = new Date(date.getTime() - date.getTimezoneOffset() * 60_000)
+  return local.toISOString().slice(0, 16)
+}
+
+function fromInputValue(value: string): string | null {
+  if (value === '') {
+    return null
+  }
+
+  // A date-time with no offset is parsed as local time.
+  const date = new Date(value)
+  return Number.isNaN(date.getTime()) ? null : date.toISOString()
+}
+
+export default function PublishAtField({ value, onChange, error, className }: Props) {
+  // The server renders in its own time zone. Local time is filled in once the
+  // page runs in the browser, so both renders of the first paint agree.
+  const [timeZone, setTimeZone] = useState<string | null>(null)
+  useEffect(() => {
+    setTimeZone(Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().timeZone)
+  }, [])
+
+  return (
+    <div>
+      <label htmlFor="publish-at" className="block text-sm font-bold text-g-heading">
+        Publish at
+      </label>
+      <input
+        id="publish-at"
+        type="datetime-local"
+        value={timeZone === null ? '' : toInputValue(value)}
+        onChange={(event) => onChange(fromInputValue(event.target.value))}
+        className={className}
+      />
+      <p className="mt-1 text-sm text-g-muted">
+        Optional{timeZone ? `, in your local time (${timeZone})` : ''}. Leave empty to publish as soon as you save.
+      </p>
+      {error ? <p className="mt-1 text-sm text-g-danger">{error}</p> : null}
+    </div>
+  )
+}
